WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.205
Federal HR 2.0, the program OPM runs,

2
00:00:03.205 --> 00:00:08.500
moves agencies' core HR records
onto one Oracle platform.

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:12.051
If an agency builds HR-driven
identity on top of it,

4
00:00:12.051 --> 00:00:17.335
so that a hire in HR becomes an account
and a departure removes one, the data

5
00:00:17.335 --> 00:00:20.280
crosses Oracle's cloud, Microsoft's cloud,

6
00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.225
the agency's own network,
and possibly AWS.

7
00:00:23.225 --> 00:00:26.950
Every one of those hops
starts with a DNS lookup.

8
00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:32.163
DNS, the domain name system, turns
a name into a network address.

9
00:00:32.163 --> 00:00:34.750
This explainer answers three questions.

10
00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:36.562
Which names must resolve where?

11
00:00:36.562 --> 00:00:37.574
What breaks?

12
00:00:37.574 --> 00:00:43.038
And when do you need private DNS or
forwarding, instead of ordinary public DNS?

13
00:00:43.038 --> 00:00:47.163
It's built for architects still
learning, so terms are defined as we go.

14
00:00:47.163 --> 00:00:48.963
This is research under review.

15
00:00:48.963 --> 00:00:53.447
Every fact comes from sources
opened on October twenty-second,

16
00:00:53.447 --> 00:00:55.949
2026, mostly vendor documentation.

17
00:00:55.949 --> 00:00:59.950
A few Microsoft facts come from
search excerpts of Microsoft Learn

18
00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:03.450
pages rather than full pages, and
I'll say so when they come up.

19
00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:08.250
Where I drew a conclusion that no source
states directly, I'll call it an inference.

20
00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:11.638
And recommendations are labeled
program design guidance.

21
00:01:11.638 --> 00:01:18.834
Sources: Research brief, DNS for
HR-driven identity across Oracle,

22
00:01:18.834 --> 00:01:24.431
Microsoft, on-premises,
VMware and AWS (verified Oct.

23
00:01:24.431 --> 00:01:27.762
22, 2026), Method and Bottom line;

24
00:01:27.762 --> 00:01:34.380
Book 03 Ch03 (OPM as the Federal
HR 2.0 program owner); Book 08.

25
00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:41.068
Here's the chain end to end, as Microsoft's
Oracle tutorial and Book 03 describe it.

26
00:01:41.068 --> 00:01:47.379
It starts in Oracle Fusion Cloud HCM,
the HR system Oracle runs as a service.

27
00:01:47.379 --> 00:01:50.143
HCM means human capital management.

28
00:01:50.143 --> 00:01:53.334
Changes leave Fusion as
extracts, or as Atom feeds,

29
00:01:53.334 --> 00:01:57.304
which are lists of recent changes
that another program polls.

30
00:01:57.304 --> 00:02:00.292
In this pattern, the
middleware pulls from Oracle.

31
00:02:00.292 --> 00:02:02.654
The middleware is the
program in the middle.

32
00:02:02.654 --> 00:02:07.550
Microsoft says it can run in Oracle
Integration Cloud, called OIC,

33
00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:12.717
or in Azure as an Azure Function, a
Logic App, or a Data Factory pipeline.

34
00:02:12.717 --> 00:02:17.655
It turns HR records into a standard
format called SCIM and posts them to

35
00:02:17.655 --> 00:02:22.593
Microsoft Graph, Microsoft's main API,
at an endpoint called bulkUpload.

36
00:02:22.593 --> 00:02:25.881
That's Entra's API-driven
inbound provisioning.

37
00:02:25.881 --> 00:02:30.042
The Entra provisioning service then
writes the user into Entra ID.

38
00:02:30.042 --> 00:02:32.836
For users who still live
in Active Directory,

39
00:02:32.836 --> 00:02:36.838
it hands the change to a provisioning
agent: a small service on an

40
00:02:36.838 --> 00:02:41.596
on-premises server that makes outbound
connections only, and writes into AD.

41
00:02:41.596 --> 00:02:46.655
Lifecycle Workflows then run joiner and
leaver tasks on the hire and leave dates.

42
00:02:46.655 --> 00:02:48.705
Notice the DNS pattern.

43
00:02:48.705 --> 00:02:52.317
Every arrow in the top row
crosses a vendor's public name.

44
00:02:52.317 --> 00:02:56.205
Only the agent-to-AD
hop needs internal DNS.

45
00:02:56.205 --> 00:03:03.511
Sources: Microsoft Learn: Configure Oracle
HCM for automatic user provisioning [S1],

46
00:03:03.511 --> 00:03:10.499
Perform bulkUpload [S5], On-premises
application provisioning architecture [S6

47
00:03:10.499 --> 00:03:16.958
(excerpt)], Plan cloud HR application to
Entra user provisioning [S8 (excerpt)],

48
00:03:16.958 --> 00:03:21.935
Lifecycle workflows attribute
sync [S11 (excerpt)]; Oracle,

49
00:03:21.935 --> 00:03:27.390
Working with Atom [S18]; Book
03 Ch05, Provisioning Services.

50
00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.502
That leads to the key idea of this talk.

51
00:03:30.502 --> 00:03:34.490
Almost every name in this path
is public and owned by a vendor.

52
00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:38.344
The Fusion host, which looks
like your environment's name,

53
00:03:38.344 --> 00:03:41.213
then dot f-a dot o-c-s
dot oraclecloud dot com.

54
00:03:41.213 --> 00:03:44.165
Microsoft Graph and the
Entra sign-in hosts.

55
00:03:44.165 --> 00:03:46.553
The provisioning agent's relay names.

56
00:03:46.553 --> 00:03:50.890
And the Amazon Connect names that
contact-center agents' browsers use.

57
00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:53.746
The agency's job is to
resolve those correctly

58
00:03:53.746 --> 00:03:56.377
from every resolver a component can reach.

59
00:03:56.377 --> 00:03:59.928
A resolver is the DNS
server a machine asks.

60
00:03:59.928 --> 00:04:05.190
The agency must never host or shadow
those names in a private zone of its own.

61
00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:07.178
We'll see why on the failure slide.

62
00:04:07.178 --> 00:04:11.838
Private DNS is needed, along with
forwarding, which means one resolver

63
00:04:11.838 --> 00:04:16.339
passing a query to another that knows
the answer, in only four places.

64
00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:21.494
One: names the agency owns, like AD
domains and their service records,

65
00:04:21.494 --> 00:04:27.477
the middleware's evidence store, internal
HR or identity APIs, and VMware hosts.

66
00:04:27.477 --> 00:04:32.471
Two: cloud private endpoints the middleware
uses, such as a storage account behind

67
00:04:32.471 --> 00:04:37.390
Azure Private Link, which gives a cloud
service a private address in your network.

68
00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:41.590
Three: cloud private zones that
on-premises systems must read.

69
00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:45.853
Four: AWS interface
endpoints with private DNS.

70
00:04:45.853 --> 00:04:48.891
Everything else is ordinary
public resolution.

71
00:04:48.891 --> 00:05:00.436
Sources: Research brief, Bottom line items
1 and 2, citing Microsoft Learn [S2, S4,

72
00:05:00.436 --> 00:05:07.291
S10], Oracle [S20, S23], AWS
[S27–S29, S31], Broadcom [S36];

73
00:05:07.291 --> 00:05:15.960
Book 08 Ch07 and Ch08;
research_multicloud.md [prior-M S57].

74
00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:20.723
This table condenses the brief's
what-must-resolve-where table.

75
00:05:20.723 --> 00:05:22.809
The full version is in the backup.

76
00:05:22.809 --> 00:05:28.105
The HR middleware, if it runs as Azure
Functions or Logic Apps Standard joined to a

77
00:05:28.105 --> 00:05:30.871
virtual network, resolves the Fusion host,

78
00:05:30.871 --> 00:05:34.585
Graph and sign-in publicly,
through the agency's resolver.

79
00:05:34.585 --> 00:05:38.545
Its evidence store and key vault
sit behind private endpoints,

80
00:05:38.545 --> 00:05:42.885
so it needs a linked private zone, or
a resolver that forwards to Azure.

81
00:05:42.885 --> 00:05:47.165
Microsoft documents an app
setting, WEBSITE_DNS_SERVER,

82
00:05:47.165 --> 00:05:50.422
for pointing the app at
your own DNS server.

83
00:05:50.422 --> 00:05:55.910
Lifecycle Workflow extensions run in
Consumption Logic Apps, the multitenant kind.

84
00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:59.185
As an inference, they can
reach only public names.

85
00:05:59.185 --> 00:06:04.191
The provisioning agent resolves domain
controller locator records internally,

86
00:06:04.191 --> 00:06:07.135
and Microsoft's relay and
sign-in names publicly.

87
00:06:07.135 --> 00:06:11.657
On-premises systems reach cloud private
names by forwarding from the Grid,

88
00:06:11.657 --> 00:06:15.669
meaning the Infoblox appliances,
to each cloud's inbound endpoint:

89
00:06:15.669 --> 00:06:19.973
an address inside the cloud network
that accepts queries from outside.

90
00:06:19.973 --> 00:06:25.722
Fusion users on Oracle's private access
still get Fusion's public DNS answer.

91
00:06:25.722 --> 00:06:29.286
A Lambda function in a VPC,
a virtual private cloud,

92
00:06:29.286 --> 00:06:33.372
reaches agency APIs through
an outbound rule to the Grid.

93
00:06:33.372 --> 00:06:39.386
And vCenter and ESXi need forward and
reverse records, A and PTR, in the Grid.

94
00:06:39.386 --> 00:06:50.456
Sources: Research brief §8, What must
resolve where, and the sources in each row:

95
00:06:50.456 --> 00:06:58.891
Microsoft Learn [S2, S4, S4b (excerpt),
S8 (excerpt), S11b (excerpt),

96
00:06:58.891 --> 00:07:06.974
S13–S15 (excerpts)]; Oracle [S20–S24];
AWS [S29, S30]; Broadcom [S36];

97
00:07:06.974 --> 00:07:15.950
Book 08 Ch07 and Ch08;
research_multicloud.md [prior-M S31, S57].

98
00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:19.644
Next, the government clouds
use different names,

99
00:07:19.644 --> 00:07:21.925
and getting this wrong fails quietly.

100
00:07:21.925 --> 00:07:25.000
Microsoft Graph has a
separate address per cloud.

101
00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:30.734
A GCC tenant, Microsoft's government
community cloud, keeps using the worldwide

102
00:07:30.734 --> 00:07:36.294
endpoints: graph dot microsoft dot com,
and login dot microsoftonline dot com.

103
00:07:36.294 --> 00:07:39.769
A GCC High tenant uses
graph dot microsoft dot us,

104
00:07:39.769 --> 00:07:42.462
and login dot microsoftonline dot us.

105
00:07:42.462 --> 00:07:47.199
The higher government cloud has its own
Graph host and the same dot-us sign-in.

106
00:07:47.199 --> 00:07:50.129
Microsoft says access
tokens from one national

107
00:07:50.129 --> 00:07:52.838
cloud aren't interchangeable
with another's.

108
00:07:52.838 --> 00:07:55.163
The provisioning agent has its own list.

109
00:07:55.163 --> 00:07:58.466
In the public cloud, which
GCC tenants also use,

110
00:07:58.466 --> 00:08:04.463
it reaches relay names under msappproxy
dot net and servicebus dot windows dot net.

111
00:08:04.463 --> 00:08:05.003
In the U.S.

112
00:08:05.003 --> 00:08:09.175
Government cloud those become
msappproxy dot us and servicebus

113
00:08:09.175 --> 00:08:13.425
dot usgovcloudapi dot net, with
dot-us certificate-check names too.

114
00:08:13.425 --> 00:08:18.040
The agent uses outbound port
443 for service traffic and port

115
00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:22.837
80 for certificate revocation
lists, and needs no inbound ports.

116
00:08:22.837 --> 00:08:25.543
Per Microsoft Learn, from search excerpts:

117
00:08:25.543 --> 00:08:28.944
public DNS for these names
is a chain of CNAME records,

118
00:08:28.944 --> 00:08:34.199
you should avoid inline TLS inspection,
meaning a proxy that decrypts the traffic,

119
00:08:34.199 --> 00:08:39.300
and the agent installs to the default
Azure environment unless told otherwise.

120
00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:43.750
So keep a per-cloud list of names,
and test through the real egress path.

121
00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:49.158
Sources: Microsoft Learn: Microsoft
Graph national cloud deployments [S2];

122
00:08:49.158 --> 00:08:55.275
Prerequisites for Microsoft Entra Cloud
Sync, firewall and proxy requirements [S4];

