126
00:09:06.978 --> 00:09:10.970
considerations for Azure
Government [S7 (excerpt)].

127
00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:13.970
Now Oracle, and a common mistake.

128
00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:17.896
Oracle Fusion offers private access
from on-premises, which Oracle

129
00:09:17.896 --> 00:09:22.382
describes as reaching your environment
without going through the internet.

130
00:09:22.382 --> 00:09:24.883
It sounds like a DNS feature.

131
00:09:24.883 --> 00:09:25.708
It isn't.

132
00:09:25.708 --> 00:09:26.607
It's routing.

133
00:09:26.607 --> 00:09:30.979
Traffic goes over FastConnect,
Oracle's private circuit,

134
00:09:30.979 --> 00:09:35.721
or a site-to-site VPN, into an
Oracle virtual cloud network, a VCN.

135
00:09:35.721 --> 00:09:38.881
From there it goes through
the VCN's Service Gateway

136
00:09:38.881 --> 00:09:41.895
to the Oracle Services
Network's public IP ranges.

137
00:09:41.895 --> 00:09:44.658
You add an access rule
for the VCN and turn

138
00:09:44.658 --> 00:09:47.583
off content acceleration,
Oracle's caching.

139
00:09:47.583 --> 00:09:51.545
Neither Oracle page says
anything about DNS.

140
00:09:51.545 --> 00:09:55.058
As an inference, the Fusion
name keeps its public answer.

141
00:09:55.058 --> 00:09:57.395
So don't build a private zone for Fusion.

142
00:09:57.395 --> 00:09:59.058
Second, Atom feeds.

143
00:09:59.058 --> 00:10:02.160
Oracle says Atom data can
be periodically purged,

144
00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:06.095
and Microsoft warns that delaying
Atom feeds can lose changes.

145
00:10:06.095 --> 00:10:09.955
As an inference, if the
middleware loses DNS or internet

146
00:10:09.955 --> 00:10:13.983
access for longer than the purge
window, HR events are lost.

147
00:10:13.983 --> 00:10:17.882
Middleware DNS is a correctness
issue, not just uptime.

148
00:10:17.882 --> 00:10:20.308
Reconcile against full extracts.

149
00:10:20.308 --> 00:10:21.820
Third, FedRAMP.

150
00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:25.945
The Marketplace lists Oracle
Fusion Cloud at FedRAMP Moderate.

151
00:10:25.945 --> 00:10:30.597
Oracle's government cloud regions
support private DNS zones,

152
00:10:30.597 --> 00:10:33.333
but public DNS isn't available there.

153
00:10:33.333 --> 00:10:37.869
Which Oracle environment hosts a
given agency's tenant wasn't verified.

154
00:10:37.869 --> 00:10:41.801
Sources: Oracle: OCI Fusion Applications,

155
00:10:41.801 --> 00:10:46.194
Securely Accessing Fusion
Applications [S21];

156
00:10:46.194 --> 00:10:51.858
Transit Routing for Private
Access to Oracle Services [S22];

157
00:10:51.858 --> 00:10:56.714
REST API for Fusion Cloud
HCM, Working with Atom [S18];

158
00:10:56.714 --> 00:11:03.419
US Government Cloud with FedRAMP
authorization [S26]; Microsoft Learn,

159
00:11:03.419 --> 00:11:07.003
Oracle HCM provisioning tutorial [S1];

160
00:11:07.003 --> 00:11:13.150
FedRAMP Marketplace products.json,
record F1603257882 [S40].

161
00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:19.738
Two more components: Amazon Connect,
AWS's contact center service, and VMware.

162
00:11:19.738 --> 00:11:23.878
Connect agents work in a browser,
in the Contact Control Panel,

163
00:11:23.878 --> 00:11:27.613
and AWS says that traffic
traverses the public network.

164
00:11:27.613 --> 00:11:33.629
AWS prefers a domain allowlist: the
instance name under my dot connect dot aws,

165
00:11:33.629 --> 00:11:39.363
plus telemetry, participant and transport
names, and media names for TURN,

166
00:11:39.363 --> 00:11:43.312
a relay that carries the call
audio, on UDP port 3478.

167
00:11:43.312 --> 00:11:46.356
If agents' DNS can't
resolve those TURN names,

168
00:11:46.356 --> 00:11:49.400
the softphone can't set
up its media channel.

169
00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:52.475
So don't over-restrict DNS for agents.

170
00:11:52.475 --> 00:11:57.525
In AWS GovCloud, Connect is
available only in the US-West region.

171
00:11:57.525 --> 00:12:00.726
It uses names under govcloud
dot connect dot aws,

172
00:12:00.726 --> 00:12:05.448
has no cross-partition integration
with commercial regions, and lacks

173
00:12:05.448 --> 00:12:10.650
several features, including Customer
Profiles, Cases and outbound campaigns.

174
00:12:10.650 --> 00:12:16.931
AWS PrivateLink, which gives an AWS API
a private address inside your network,

175
00:12:16.931 --> 00:12:19.875
covers only a subset of Connect APIs.

176
00:12:19.875 --> 00:12:25.725
As an inference, that helps workloads making
API calls, not the agent or voice path.

177
00:12:25.725 --> 00:12:29.430
A Lambda function attached
to a VPC can reach only

178
00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:33.225
that VPC unless you add
NAT or interface endpoints.

179
00:12:33.225 --> 00:12:38.655
VMware: a Broadcom knowledge article says
changing vCenter's hostname or address

180
00:12:38.655 --> 00:12:41.130
runs forward and reverse DNS lookups,

181
00:12:41.130 --> 00:12:44.963
and the change is blocked if
the name doesn't fully resolve.

182
00:12:44.963 --> 00:12:46.937
Keep those records right in the Grid.

183
00:12:46.937 --> 00:12:53.754
Sources: AWS: Amazon Connect, Set
up your network for the CCP [S27];

184
00:12:53.754 --> 00:12:59.100
AWS GovCloud (US) User Guide,
Connect Customer [S28];

185
00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:05.648
Amazon Connect and interface
VPC endpoints [S29]; AWS Lambda,

186
00:13:05.648 --> 00:13:11.127
Giving functions access to
resources in a VPC [S30];

187
00:13:11.127 --> 00:13:17.630
Broadcom KB 440783, VAMI hostname
or IP change fails [S36].

188
00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:19.655
Here's the subtle one.

189
00:13:19.655 --> 00:13:22.052
Suppose a cloud network has both a private

190
00:13:22.052 --> 00:13:24.517
zone and a forwarding
rule for the same name.

191
00:13:24.517 --> 00:13:25.556
Which wins?

192
00:13:25.556 --> 00:13:27.305
The three clouds disagree.

193
00:13:27.305 --> 00:13:33.130
In AWS, the Route 53 Resolver rule
wins over the private hosted zone.

194
00:13:33.130 --> 00:13:37.932
In Azure, a linked private DNS zone
wins, and, per Microsoft Learn,

195
00:13:37.932 --> 00:13:41.718
the forwarding-ruleset
rule is silently ignored.

196
00:13:41.718 --> 00:13:46.504
In Oracle Cloud, the attached private
views are checked before resolver rules.

197
00:13:46.504 --> 00:13:51.230
Once a view answers, evaluation stops,
even if the answer is negative.

198
00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:52.942
That word negative matters.

199
00:13:52.942 --> 00:13:54.980
A private zone can shadow a name.

200
00:13:54.980 --> 00:13:59.610
If the zone covers a name but has no
record for it, it returns NXDOMAIN,

201
00:13:59.610 --> 00:14:03.992
which means the name doesn't exist,
instead of asking anyone else.

202
00:14:03.992 --> 00:14:06.643
All three clouds document that behavior.

203
00:14:06.643 --> 00:14:09.279
So, as an inference, the same zone and the

204
00:14:09.279 --> 00:14:12.492
same rule give different
answers in each cloud.

205
00:14:12.492 --> 00:14:16.518
The only design that behaves the same
everywhere is one owner per name.

206
00:14:16.518 --> 00:14:18.681
The Grid owns agency zones.

207
00:14:18.681 --> 00:14:21.105
Each cloud owns its own private zones.

208
00:14:21.105 --> 00:14:23.217
Everything else forwards to the owner.

209
00:14:23.217 --> 00:14:26.839
Never put a zone and a rule for
the same name in one network,

210
00:14:26.839 --> 00:14:29.304
and test resolution from every origin,

211
00:14:29.304 --> 00:14:33.618
because a configuration that works
in one cloud can fail in the next.

212
00:14:33.618 --> 00:14:41.003
Sources: AWS, Route 53, Considerations
when working with a private hosted zone

213
00:14:41.003 --> 00:14:48.504
[S25]; Microsoft Learn, Troubleshoot
NXDOMAIN from an Azure Private DNS zone [S12

214
00:14:48.504 --> 00:14:51.851
(excerpt)]; Oracle, OCI Private DNS [S23];

215
00:14:51.851 --> 00:14:56.840
research brief, Bottom line
4 and failure mode F3.

216
00:14:57.590 --> 00:14:58.715
How does this break?

217
00:14:58.715 --> 00:15:02.065
Here are seven failures from
the brief's table of sixteen.

218
00:15:02.065 --> 00:15:04.090
The full table is in the backup.

219
00:15:04.090 --> 00:15:05.290
One, shadowing.

220
00:15:05.290 --> 00:15:08.750
Someone creates a private
zone for a vendor's domain,

221
00:15:08.750 --> 00:15:13.740
say oraclecloud dot com, and every name
not copied into it returns NXDOMAIN.

222
00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:15.452
Two, silent fallback.

223
00:15:15.452 --> 00:15:18.931
A private endpoint name
resolves to the public address,

224
00:15:18.931 --> 00:15:21.728
because a forwarding
rule is missing or slow.

225
00:15:21.728 --> 00:15:23.623
Per a Microsoft Learn excerpt,

226
00:15:23.623 --> 00:15:27.868
Windows DNS servers that forward
to Azure need a forwarder timeout

227
00:15:27.868 --> 00:15:32.341
above four seconds, or private records
may resolve to public addresses.

228
00:15:32.341 --> 00:15:34.552
Three, the Grid's own fallback.

229
00:15:34.552 --> 00:15:39.186
Infoblox documents that if Use Forwarders
Only is off and the forwarders fail,

230
00:15:39.186 --> 00:15:43.178
the member falls back to resolving
from the internet's root servers.

231
00:15:43.178 --> 00:15:45.530
As an inference, a failed forward can then

232
00:15:45.530 --> 00:15:48.090
return the public answer
instead of failing.

233
00:15:48.090 --> 00:15:49.165
Four, loops.

234
00:15:49.165 --> 00:15:52.907
Azure warns that a ruleset rule
that targets an inbound endpoint

235
00:15:52.907 --> 00:15:56.303
must not be linked to the network
where that endpoint lives.

236
00:15:56.303 --> 00:15:58.816
Five, TLS name mismatch.

237
00:15:58.816 --> 00:16:01.674
Keep the public name in
every connection string,

238
00:16:01.674 --> 00:16:04.390
because the service's
certificate carries it.

239
00:16:04.390 --> 00:16:09.228
Six, the wrong cloud's endpoints, as
we saw on the government-cloud slide.

240
00:16:09.228 --> 00:16:10.803
Seven, capacity.

241
00:16:10.803 --> 00:16:15.835
A Route 53 Resolver endpoint address handles
about ten thousand queries per second,

242
00:16:15.835 --> 00:16:19.141
but as few as fifteen hundred
with connection tracking.

243
00:16:19.141 --> 00:16:22.178
Size endpoints before
each onboarding wave.

244
00:16:22.178 --> 00:16:31.793
Sources: Research brief §6, failure
modes F1, F4, F5, F6, F9, F10, F11:

245
00:16:31.793 --> 00:16:43.175
Microsoft Learn [S2, S10 (excerpt), S10b
(excerpt), S12 (excerpt), S14b (excerpt)];

246
00:16:43.175 --> 00:16:49.454
AWS [S25, S32]; Oracle
[S23]; Infoblox NIOS 9.1,

247
00:16:49.454 --> 00:16:57.670
Specifying Forwarders [S37];
Book 08 Ch02 and Ch10.

248
00:16:58.420 --> 00:17:01.320
With several clouds, how do
you wire the forwarding?

249
00:17:01.320 --> 00:17:03.157
Every vendor documents a hub.

250
00:17:03.157 --> 00:17:04.732
None recommends a mesh.

251
00:17:04.732 --> 00:17:10.277
Azure documents two hub designs: link one
forwarding ruleset to every spoke network,

252
00:17:10.277 --> 00:17:13.445
or point the spokes at the
hub's inbound endpoint.

253
00:17:13.445 --> 00:17:18.595
AWS shares Resolver rules from one
account through Resource Access Manager.

254
00:17:18.595 --> 00:17:21.920
Oracle shares a view across
networks in a region.

255
00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:24.058
Here's some illustrative arithmetic.

256
00:17:24.058 --> 00:17:30.795
Take five environments: on-premises,
Azure, AWS, Oracle Cloud and VMware.

257
00:17:30.795 --> 00:17:34.557
In a full mesh, each forwards
directly to every other,

258
00:17:34.557 --> 00:17:38.582
so you need up to twenty one-way
rule sets, five times four.

259
00:17:38.582 --> 00:17:43.082
With a hub, you need about five, one
per environment pointing at the hub.

260
00:17:43.082 --> 00:17:47.517
As an inference, every extra path is
one more place for the precedence,

261
00:17:47.517 --> 00:17:50.007
fallback and loop failures we just saw.

262
00:17:50.007 --> 00:17:51.658
Where does Infoblox fit?

263
00:17:51.658 --> 00:17:54.933
The Grid is a natural
hub for agency names.

264
00:17:54.933 --> 00:17:58.956
Each Grid member can have its own
forwarders for a forward zone,

265
00:17:58.956 --> 00:18:03.212
and Infoblox's reference design
puts vNIOS, its virtual appliance,

266
00:18:03.212 --> 00:18:06.307
in a shared-services hub
network as a Grid member.

267
00:18:06.307 --> 00:18:10.122
The earlier multi-cloud research,
labeled as design guidance,

268
00:18:10.122 --> 00:18:14.311
recommends a baseline of the Grid
plus each cloud's native resolver,

269
00:18:14.311 --> 00:18:19.621
forwarding both ways, with customer-operated
appliances for FedRAMP High workloads,

270
00:18:19.621 --> 00:18:24.333
because Infoblox's software-as-a-service
offering is FedRAMP Moderate only.

271
00:18:24.333 --> 00:18:32.489
Sources: Microsoft Learn, Azure DNS Private
Resolver endpoints and rulesets [S10

272
00:18:32.489 --> 00:18:39.550
(excerpt)] and Private resolver architecture
[S10c (excerpt)]; AWS, Route 53,

273
00:18:39.550 --> 00:18:47.097
Managing forwarding rules [S33]; Oracle,
OCI Private DNS [S23]; Infoblox NIOS 9.1,

274
00:18:47.097 --> 00:18:52.697
Configuring a Forward Zone
[S38]; research_multicloud.md,

275
00:18:52.697 --> 00:18:59.879
Bottom line and Option A (Infoblox
Shared-Services VPC Deployments [S27]);

276
00:18:59.879 --> 00:19:04.740
mesh-versus-hub arithmetic
is illustrative.

277
00:19:05.490 --> 00:19:07.140
So what must the agency do?

278
00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:10.646
This is program design guidance,
not a vendor requirement,

279
00:19:10.646 --> 00:19:13.202
condensed from a thirteen-step checklist.

280
00:19:13.202 --> 00:19:14.965
Names and owners first.

281
00:19:14.965 --> 00:19:18.853
Inventory every name in
the HR path, per cloud.

282
00:19:18.853 --> 00:19:21.777
Never create a private zone
for a vendor's domain.

283
00:19:21.777 --> 00:19:26.277
Give each agency namespace one
owner, and forward everything else.

284
00:19:26.277 --> 00:19:31.115
And never let a zone and a rule for
the same name exist in one network.

285
00:19:31.115 --> 00:19:31.953
Forwarding.

286
00:19:31.953 --> 00:19:37.267
From the Grid, forward each Azure service's
public zone, not its privatelink zone,

287
00:19:37.267 --> 00:19:42.502
to inbound endpoints in two regions, and
set Use Forwarders Only on those zones.

288
00:19:42.502 --> 00:19:46.227
Raise Windows forwarder
timeouts above four seconds.

289
00:19:46.227 --> 00:19:51.965
Run a hub in each cloud, and review the
forwarding graph for loops at every change.

290
00:19:51.965 --> 00:19:57.053
Size resolver endpoints before
each HR 2.0 onboarding wave.

291
00:19:57.053 --> 00:19:58.090
Components.

292
00:19:58.090 --> 00:20:03.165
Put the middleware in a Logic Apps Standard
or Functions plan joined to a virtual

293
00:20:03.165 --> 00:20:05.665
network, using the agency resolver path,

294
00:20:05.665 --> 00:20:09.377
and keep Lifecycle Workflow
extensions to public targets.

295
00:20:09.377 --> 00:20:13.738
Run at least two provisioning agents
dedicated to HR provisioning,

296
00:20:13.738 --> 00:20:17.553
near preferred domain controllers,
with no TLS inspection.

297
00:20:17.553 --> 00:20:21.104
Treat middleware DNS as
a data-integrity control,

298
00:20:21.104 --> 00:20:23.789
and reconcile against full extracts.

299
00:20:23.789 --> 00:20:26.465
For Connect, let the TURN names resolve.

300
00:20:26.465 --> 00:20:30.153
For VMware, keep A and
PTR records in the Grid.

301
00:20:30.153 --> 00:20:32.066
And test from every origin.

302
00:20:32.066 --> 00:20:36.278
Any public answer or NXDOMAIN
for a private name is a defect.

303
00:20:36.278 --> 00:20:41.011
Sources: Research brief §9,

304
00:20:41.011 --> 00:20:55.885
Agency checklist steps 1–13 (all PROGRAM
DESIGN GUIDANCE) and the facts each step

305
